<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Network posture for WREACT.

  MASVS-NETWORK-1: all traffic is TLS. cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" is the
  platform default at targetSdk 36, but declaring it explicitly means a future
  library that ships its own manifest cannot quietly re-enable it, and it makes
  the intent auditable rather than implied.

  On certificate pinning, deliberately omitted, with reasoning rather than a
  shrug: the app talks to Railway (wreact-production.up.railway.app) and Google
  (Firebase auth, Firestore, FCM). Both rotate leaf and intermediate
  certificates on their own schedule and neither publishes a pin set, so a
  hardcoded pin is a scheduled outage — when it expires the app cannot reach its
  own backend and no server-side change can fix an already-installed build.

  It would also buy very little here. Pinning defends confidentiality and
  integrity of the channel; this app's threat is a player forging their own
  scores, and that player owns the device, so they can read the traffic whatever
  we pin. The defence against forged scores is server-side validation and
  attestation of the caller, not making the wire harder to read.

  If pinning is wanted later, pin to the CA's SPKI rather than the leaf, always
  ship at least one backup pin, and set an expiration so a missed rotation
  degrades to unpinned instead of bricking the app.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!-- System CAs only. User-installed CAs are deliberately not
                 trusted, so a proxy CA added on a rooted device or emulator
                 (Burp, mitmproxy, Charles) cannot transparently intercept
                 traffic from a release build. This is the pinning-adjacent
                 control that costs nothing to maintain. -->
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!-- Debug builds keep user CAs so the traffic stays inspectable during
         development. This block is ignored in a release build. -->
    <debug-overrides>
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
